 "result": "This is a Flask-style fork (package `tessel`) with renamed APIs.\n\n**How `request`, `session` and `g` get in**\n\n1. **Jinja globals:** `Tessel.create_jinja_environment` in `src/tessel/app.py:467` adds `request`, `session`, `g` (plus `config`, `link_for`, `get_flashed_messages`) to `rv.globals` (lines 493–503). The proxies are therefore visible even in imported templates.\n2. **Default injector:** `_base_template_vars` in `src/tessel/templating.py:21` replaces `g` and `request` with the concrete objects for faster access. `session` stays a proxy so that `session.accessed` is tracked. It is pre-registered in `template_ctx_injectors[None]` (`src/tessel/sansio/scaffold.py:182-184`).\n3. **At render time:** `render_template` calls `_render` (`templating.py:123`), which calls `app.fill_template_scope(ctx, context)` (`src/tessel/app.py:588`). That function runs every injector for the app scope (`None`) and for the current request's bundles, then re-applies the context you passed to `render_template` so your values win.\n\n**Adding your own variables**\n\n- Decorate a function that returns a dict with **`@app.ctx_injector`**. It is defined in `src/tessel/sansio/scaffold.py:550`. This is Flask's `context_processor`, renamed.\n- On a bundle, `@bundle.ctx_injector` applies only to templates rendered from that bundle's views.\n- `@bundle.app_ctx_injector` (`src/tessel/sansio/bundles.py:~651`) applies the injector app-wide.\n\n```python\n@app.ctx_injector\ndef inject():\n    return {\"site_name\": \"Tessel\"}\n```",
